# Key Concepts — Lecture 01 (Introduction) + Lindsay Ch. 1

Entries are grouped thematically and are meant to stand alone. Source is given as `[L1: slide n]` for the lecture deck and `[Lindsay Ch1]` for the reading.

## Conceptual prerequisites for this lecture

You will get more out of it if you already have: basic probability (probability distributions, conditional probability); the idea of a likelihood function; linear regression and least squares; the notion of an optimisation/minimisation problem; and Python basics (functions, loops, NumPy arrays, `scipy.optimize`). No neuroscience background is assumed, but familiarity with a classic paradigm (e.g. a two-alternative forced-choice task or a bandit task) helps.

---

## A. What a model is

**Computational model** — A quantitative simulation of a natural phenomenon, usually implemented as code. `[L1: 13]`
Not specific to psychology: weather forecasting, protein folding, chess engines and the ideal gas law all qualify. What makes something a model is that it takes inputs and parameters and produces outputs you can compare to reality. The key move is that the theory is stated precisely enough that a computer, which has no intuitions, can execute it.

**Cognitive model** — A formal (equation-based) model that mimics the intelligent behaviour of humans or animals; a mathematical description of the internal processes producing behavioural or physiological phenomena. `[L1: 14; Nassar & Frank, 2016]`
The distinguishing feature is the _target_: natural cognition, not just performance. A model that beats humans at a task without behaving like one is an engineering success and a cognitive-modelling failure.

**Model as a quantitative stand-in for a theory** — The claim that the model does not _describe_ your theory but _is_ your theory in runnable form. `[L1: 28]`
Consequence: criticising the model is criticising the theory, and a model that cannot be made to run reveals a theory that was never complete.

**"A narrative is not a model"** — Verbal theories can hide internal inconsistencies, logical contradictions and gaps that formalisation exposes; a running model is a sufficiency proof that the ideas cohere. `[L1: 17, 28; Fum, Del Missier & Stocco, 2007]`
Example: "working memory holds 7 ± 2 items" sounds precise but does not specify what an item is, how items are encoded, or what happens at capacity. Three modellers would build three different models from it.

**Wiener's cat / spherical cow** — The observation that a perfect model of a system is the system itself, and therefore useless for understanding; all useful models are deliberate simplifications. `[L1: 27–28; Lindsay Ch1]`
Wiener (1945): the best material model of a cat is another, preferably the same, cat. Lindsay's "spherical cow" is the same idea told as a physics joke. _Synonymous framings:_ abstraction trade-off, the map–territory problem, "all models are wrong but some are useful" (Box).

**Level of abstraction** — The grain at which a model represents the system: synapses, cells, circuits, networks, systems, behaviour. `[L1: 34, 42]`
There is no globally correct level; the right level is the one that is useful for the phenomenon you are explaining. Choosing it is the modeller's first substantive decision and the main thing you must justify in a lab report.

