## B. Types of model

**Descriptive (statistical) model** — A model that quantifies relationships in data without claiming to reproduce the process that generated them. `[L1: 29]`
Examples: linear regression, the general linear model, factor models, most of supervised ML. Appropriate when the generating process is unknown or unmeasurable. _Weakness:_ it tells you a relationship exists, not why. Relationships per se are not explanations.

**Process (mechanistic) model** — A program that mimics the data-generating process itself, rather than describing the data. `[L1: 32, 48]`
Written so that it (i) is inspired by brain mechanisms, (ii) has parameters that vary across individuals and groups, (iii) produces data directly comparable to human data. Canonical example: `Q(a)_new = Q(a)_old + α(R − Q(a)_old)`. _Synonyms:_ mechanistic model, generative model (loosely), simulation model.

**Inference ticket** — A pejorative label for a theoretical construct that licenses inferences without doing explanatory work. `[L1: 31]`
Example given in the lecture: _g_ in factor models of intelligence — extracted from the covariance of test scores, then used to "explain" those same scores. A warning about circularity in descriptive modelling.

**Latent variable** — A quantity that cannot be observed directly but is inferred from behaviour by fitting a model. `[L1: 51]`
Examples from this course: learning rate, response caution (decision threshold), perceptual sensitivity (drift rate). This is why process models double as _measurement instruments_: they convert behaviour into psychologically interpretable numbers.

**Free parameter** — A quantity in the model not fixed by theory, whose value is estimated from data. `[L1: 51, 61, 64]`
More free parameters means more flexibility, better fit, and worse generalisation — hence the penalty terms in AIC/BIC.

