## C. Levels and traditions

**Marr's three levels** — Computational theory (what is the goal of the computation and why is it appropriate?), representation and algorithm (how is it implemented — what representations, what algorithm?), hardware implementation (how is it physically realised?). `[L1: 41; Marr, 1982]`
The levels are semi-independent: the same computation can be achieved by different algorithms, and the same algorithm by different hardware. Most cognitive models in this course live at the algorithmic level while borrowing constraints from the other two.

**Newell's timescale of human action** — A hierarchy of explanatory levels ordered by the timescale of the phenomena, from millisecond neural activity to large-scale brain dynamics to choice behaviour. `[L1: 34]`
The temporal counterpart of Marr's functional hierarchy; cognitive modelling mostly targets the behaviour/cognition bands.

**Reverse-engineering vs. engineering the brain** — Reverse-engineering (psychology, neuroscience) aims to understand existing natural intelligence; engineering (AI, ML) aims to build intelligence in silico. `[L1: 33]`
Cognitive modelling borrows tools freely from the engineering side while keeping the reverse-engineering goal, which is why "it fits the data" is never sufficient here.

**Behaviourism / cognitivism / connectionism / Bayesianism** — Four successive (and overlapping) paradigms in the history of the search for a unified theory of mind. `[L1: 38]`
Roughly: behaviourism (Pavlov, Watson, Skinner; ~1900–1960) refuses to model internal states; cognitivism (Marr, Chomsky, Posner, Shallice; ~1960s–) posits internal symbolic representations and processing stages; connectionism (Hinton, McClelland; ~1980s–) explains cognition via learned distributed representations in networks; Bayesianism (Tenenbaum, Friston; ~2000s–) casts cognition as probabilistic inference under uncertainty. Each remains alive; the course samples several.

**Start from the mind vs. start from the brain** — Two modelling traditions. Mind-first models use explicit symbols and their manipulation (interpretable, easy to fit); brain-first models take biology as the existence proof of intelligence and use deep neural networks (very powerful, hard to interpret). `[L1: 44, 49; Bermúdez, 2014]`

**Normative model** — A model specifying what an _optimal_ agent would do given the structure of the task, used as a benchmark or as a hypothesis about behaviour. `[L1: 40]`
The rational-analysis logic (cf. Anderson): evolution favours effective behaviour, optimal inference is Bayesian, therefore assume people invert the task's generative model. Attractive because it can require no free parameters. _Weakness:_ optimality is only defined relative to a chosen objective and set of constraints, so "people are optimal" often smuggles in an assumption dressed as a finding.

**Cost-function approach** — Rather than hand-designing mechanisms, specify the optimisation objective (minimise classification/reconstruction error, maximise reward, plus intrinsic costs) and let a sufficiently powerful network learn the computations by function approximation. `[L1: 46; Marblestone et al.]`
The dominant strategy in deep-learning-based models of the brain. _Trade-off:_ maximal predictive power, minimal interpretability.

