## D. Fitting, comparison, uncertainty

**Model fitting** — Finding the parameter values that make model output resemble observed data as closely as possible. Requires three ingredients: a model that generates data from parameters, a cost function, and an optimisation algorithm. `[L1: 52]`

**Cost function (objective function)** — A number quantifying the mismatch between model predictions and data, which the optimiser minimises. `[L1: 52–54]`
Candidates ranked in the lecture: Pearson correlation and % variance accounted for (weakest), SSE and RMSE (better), likelihood (best). _Synonyms:_ loss function, objective function, error function.

**Likelihood** — The probability of the observed data given the model and a particular set of parameter values, viewed as a function of the parameters. `[L1: 53–55]`
Note the direction: it is _not_ the probability of the parameters. In practice one works with the **log-likelihood** (sums are more numerically stable than products) and minimises its negative.

**Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)** — Choosing the parameter values that maximise the likelihood of the observed data. `[L1: 55; Myung, 2003]`
Preferred because of **sufficiency** (the estimator captures the information the data carry about the parameter), **consistency** (the true generating value is recovered asymptotically), and **parameter invariance** (the solution does not depend on how the model was parameterised). Least-squares estimation is described in the lecture as a hack that is nonetheless correct for linear regression.

**Non-convex optimisation / local minima** — A cost landscape with multiple valleys, so that a downhill search can settle in a suboptimal one. `[L1: 58]`
Aggravated by expensive evaluations (>1 s), noisy evaluations, and roughness from numerical approximation. Practical defence: multiple random starting points, sensible bounds, grid search (`scipy.optimize.brute`).

**Hard bounds vs. plausible bounds** — Hard bounds exclude impossible parameter values (e.g. a learning rate outside [0,1]); plausible bounds mark the range you expect. `[L1: 60; Wilson & Collins, 2019]`
A best-fitting estimate sitting exactly on a bound is a diagnostic warning, not a result.

**Goodness of fit** — How closely a fitted model reproduces the observed data. `[L1: 61]`
Necessary but _not sufficient_: a good fit only qualifies a model as a candidate for further scrutiny.

**Parsimony** — Preferring the simplest model that does not fit significantly worse than more complex alternatives. `[L1: 61]`

**Generalisability** — The model's ability to account for new, unseen data. `[L1: 61]`
The quantity you actually care about, and the one that peaks and then declines as complexity grows.

**Overfitting** — Fitting noise rather than signal, so that goodness of fit keeps improving while generalisability declines. `[L1: 62; Pitt & Myung, 2002]`
The gap between the goodness-of-fit and generalisability curves in the Pitt & Myung figure _is_ overfitting.

**Penalized likelihood (AIC, BIC)** — Model-comparison criteria that add a penalty for the number of free parameters to the maximum log-likelihood. `[L1: 63–64; Vandekerckhove et al., 2015]`
`AIC = −2 log p(Y|X, θ̂) + 2k`; `BIC = −2 log p(Y|X, θ̂) + k log n`, with `k` parameters and `n` trials. Smaller is better. BIC penalises complexity more as data accumulate, so it is more conservative. Each is valid only under assumptions that are frequently unmet in practice.

**Cross-validation** — Fitting on part of the data and evaluating prediction on held-out data; typically 10-fold or leave-one-out. `[L1: 63, 65]`
Takes the model's actual structure into account rather than merely counting parameters, at higher computational cost. The ML-side counterpart of penalized likelihoods.

**Bootstrapping** — Repeatedly resampling the data with replacement, refitting each time, and using the resulting spread of estimates as a sampling distribution for standard errors and confidence intervals. `[L1: 68–72]`
Useful precisely because traditional interval estimates are only asymptotically correct, and cognitive datasets are often small per subject.

**Parameter recovery** — Simulating data from known parameter values, fitting the model to that synthetic data, and checking whether the true values are recovered. `[L1: 69–72, 77]`
If you cannot recover your own ground truth, any parameter estimates from real data are uninterpretable. Step 4 of the five simple rules.

**Model identifiability** — Whether distinct models (or distinct parameter settings) can be told apart on the basis of the data you have. `[L1: 48, 75, 77]`
Related question from slide 75: which _specific pattern_ in the data discriminates between competing models? If none does, the experiment cannot settle the question no matter how good the fitting is.

**Falsification / absolute model fit** — Testing whether the winning model can actually generate data resembling the observed data, rather than only beating its rivals. `[L1: 75–76; Palminteri, Wyart & Koechlin, 2017]`
Relative comparison alone can crown the least-bad member of a set of uniformly wrong models.

