## E. How the concepts connect

```
                        theory (verbal)
                              │ formalise
                              ▼
                     model (runnable code)
                     ╱                  ╲
        descriptive ╱                    ╲ process/mechanistic
   (what relation?)                        (what mechanism?)
                     ╲                  ╱
                      ▼                ▼
        cost function + optimiser  →  fitted parameters (latent variables)
                              │
                ┌─────────────┼──────────────┐
                ▼             ▼              ▼
       goodness of fit   parsimony     generalisability
       (necessary, not   (AIC/BIC)     (cross-validation)
        sufficient)
                              │
                              ▼
             falsification / absolute fit / parameter recovery
                              │
                              ▼
                    revise the model space  ──► back to top
```

- **Abstraction level → model type.** Deciding how much of the cat to keep (Wiener/Lindsay) determines whether you end up with a descriptive or a process model, and at which Marr level.
- **Model type → cost function.** Descriptive models often use least squares; process models generally need likelihoods, because they specify a probability of each observed response.
- **Free parameters → complexity → overfitting.** This chain is why fit alone cannot select models and why AIC/BIC and cross-validation exist.
- **Model space (slide 37) → model comparison.** Comparison is only ever _relative to the models you wrote down_; falsification (Palminteri) is the check on that blind spot.
- **Bayesianism appears twice** — once as a historical paradigm (slide 38) and once as a source of models via the normative stance (slide 40). Distinguish "Bayesian model of cognition" (a claim about the mind) from "Bayesian model fitting" (a statistical method for estimating parameters of any model). The lecture uses both senses.

## Terminology mapping (same idea, different names)

| Term used here    | Also called                                                    |
| ----------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Process model     | Mechanistic model, generative model, simulation model          |
| Cost function     | Objective function, loss function, error function              |
| Descriptive model | Statistical model, phenomenological model, curve-fitting model |
| Free parameter    | Fitted parameter, latent parameter, degree of freedom          |
| Prediction error  | Surprise, RPE (reward prediction error), δ (delta)             |
| Response caution  | Decision threshold, boundary separation, _a_ (in the DDM)      |
| Generalisability  | Out-of-sample predictive accuracy, predictive performance      |
| Spherical cow     | Toy model, minimal model, idealisation, abstraction            |
